CRITERION 5 — doc claims true as written: PASS, with one noted nuance
  Read: internal/services/retirement/settings.go:385-432 (Load), 434-438 (LoadContext),
  568-582 (loadInternal/loadInternalContext), 625-627 (LoadScenarioSettings).
  New claim: "the manager's cached object never escapes the manager, full stop — not through
  Load, and not through any mutator's return value either (every mutator that saves also
  returns prepare.Clone(settings), not settings itself)."
  Verified against criterion 3's audit: no mutator exception remains, and all three of
  LoadContext's return points clone (448, 461, 473). The parenthetical is literally true for
  all 24 save-and-return methods.
  NUANCE (pre-existing, unchanged by X2, not a mutator exception): Save/SaveWithRevision adopt
  the CALLER's pointer as the cache (saveInternal, settings.go:801 `sm.cache = settings`), so
  after a Save the caller still holds the object that is now cached. That is ingress, not
  escape, and the same comment's next sentences state the contract that covers it ("hand it
  back to Save/SaveWithRevision ... once saveInternal stores a pointer in sm.cache, nothing
  mutates that object again"). Reporting it so the lead can judge whether "full stop" overreaches.

